# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Accessibility

What this project guarantees about accessibility, what it mechanically checks, and — the section
that makes the rest trustworthy — **what it does not check at all**.

Read by `/audit-accessibility`, which takes its enforced minimums from here and from the audit
config. Those two must agree; the config is what the scanner reads, this document is what a human
reads, and a mismatch is a defect rather than a formatting nit.

Owned by whoever owns the presentation layer. Must change whenever a check is added, removed, or
turned off.

---

## 1. The three-way split

Everything below is filed under exactly one of three headings, and the split is the whole point of
this document:

| | Means | A reader may conclude |
|---|---|---|
| **Guaranteed** | Structurally true — the code cannot easily produce a violation, because the shared layer enforces it | It holds without checking each case |
| **Checked** | Not structurally guaranteed, but a check runs and fails on violation | It holds where the check reaches |
| **Not checked** | Nobody verifies this, by tooling or by process | **Nothing.** It may be fine; it has not been established |

A document listing only guarantees manufactures exactly the false confidence this split exists to
prevent: silence about a criterion reads as compliance with it. **If a criterion is not in the first
two lists, it belongs in the third by name.** An empty "not checked" section is a claim that this
project verifies everything, which is not true of any project.

